+# app
+
+A small user store on an in-memory database. Run the tests with `npm test`.
+
+## Users API
+
+Every function takes a `db` as its first argument and throws `Error('db is required')` when it is missing. Users have the shape `{ id, email, fullName, createdAt, lastLogin, nickname }`. `lastLogin` is `null` until `recordLogin` is called, and `nickname` is `null` unless one is given to `createUser`. The `nickname` column must exist in the `users` table for this to work.
+
+Emails must contain an `@` and are unique, ignoring case. Full names must be non-blank and at most 100 characters.
+
+### `src/api/users.js`
+
+- **`createUser(db, { email, fullName })`** creates a user and returns it. Throws if the email is invalid or already taken, or if the full name is invalid.
+- **`getUser(db, id)`** returns the user with this id, or `null`.
+- **`getUserByEmail(db, email)`** returns the user with this email, ignoring case, or `null`.
+- **`updateUser(db, id, { email, fullName })`** changes only the fields given, with the same checks as `createUser`. Returns the updated user, or `null` if no user has this id.
+- **`searchUsers(db, query)`** returns users whose full name or email contains `query`, ignoring case, oldest first.
+- **`deleteUser(db, id)`** removes the user. Returns `true` if one was removed, `false` otherwise.
+- **`recordLogin(db, id, when)`** sets `lastLogin`. `when` defaults to now. Returns the updated user, or `null` if no user has this id.
+- **`countUsers(db)`** returns the number of users.
+- **`listUsers(db, { limit, offset })`** returns one page of users, oldest first. `limit` defaults to 50 and `offset` to 0.
+
+### `src/api/format.js`
+
+- **`displayName(user)`** returns the full name, or the part of the email before `@` when the full name is blank.
+
+### `src/api/export.js`
+
+- **`exportUsersCsv(db)`** returns every user as CSV, with the header `id,email,fullName,createdAt,lastLogin` and one row per user, oldest first. Fields containing commas, quotes or line breaks are quoted.
+- **`importUsersCsv(db, csv)`** creates a user for each data row of a CSV in the export format and returns the created users. Each row goes through `createUser`, so the same checks apply. The database assigns `id`, `createdAt` and `lastLogin`, and the file's values for them are ignored. Rows are created one at a time, so a failure leaves the earlier rows in place. Throws if the header doesn't match.
